#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Score a skill's behaviour evals (``evals.json``) and report ship/no-ship.

Opt-in per skill: a skill is scored only if it ships ``evals/score-evals.json``.
Without that file the skill is skipped cleanly, so this tool stays runnable
across every skill rather than erroring on the ones that never opted in. Once
the config exists, a file it declares but that is missing is a hard error.

What it does, in one pass:

1. Materializes two arms into a scratch workspace — ``new/`` from the working
   tree and ``base/`` from a git ref — expanding the config's ``skill_paths``
   prefixes against each arm separately, because the two arms genuinely carry
   different ``references/`` filenames and counts.
2. Dispatches one blind runner per (chunk x arm) through a coding-agent CLI.
   The rule text is inlined into the prompt, the working directory is empty,
   and the cases handed over carry ``id`` and ``prompt`` only — nothing the
   harness sends a runner names an expectation or points at ``evals/``.
   How firm that is differs by family: ``claude`` runs with ``--tools=`` and
   can read nothing at all, while ``codex`` runs under ``-s read-only``, which
   restricts writes and not reads — that runner keeps filesystem access, and
   the inlined SKILL.md frontmatter names the skill, so a determined agent
   could go looking for the fixture. The blinding is in what is supplied, not
   in a sandbox, on the codex side.
3. Dispatches one blind grader per chunk — ``codex`` by default, same as the
   runner, since 2026-08-07 — with both arms' outputs labelled A/B under a
   per-run mapping, that chunk's scoring key, the source prompts the outputs
   came from, and the protocol's criteria section verbatim. The grader never
   sees the skill, the version labels, or any previous results file. Runner
   and grader defaulting to the same family trades away cross-family
   blinding (a same-family grader has a better chance of recognizing its own
   phrasing echoed back) for lower cost and no dependency on a second CLI
   being installed; pass ``--grader claude`` for the older cross-family
   default.
4. Reconciles and reports. The verdict class of every row is decided here,
   not by the grader, so the "zero protection-class false kills" gate means
   the same thing in every chunk and every run.

Everything runs through coding-agent CLIs (``codex exec`` / ``claude -p``);
this repo never spends a billed vendor API key on eval dispatch.

Rebuilding the bank and recalibrating
-------------------------------------

A chunk-layout change, a grader-family change, or an edited rule blob
invalidates the baseline bank, the null pool and ``calibration.json`` together.
Rebuild them in one pass, since each step's output feeds the next:

1. ``--build-bank --rounds 6`` against a pinned commit hash, never ``HEAD``.
   ``HEAD`` compares a committed change against itself once it lands, and
   ``baseline_ref`` stores the literal string, so hash rounds and ``HEAD``
   rounds refuse to pool.
2. ``--null-sweep 6`` — every pair of bank rounds, ``C(6,2)`` = 15 at six
   rounds, batched. (``--null-run A,B`` still scores a single pair by hand.)
3. ``--calibrate`` over the 15 null results.

Step 2 dominates the wall clock, and the reason is worth knowing: ``--jobs``
parallelizes chunks *within* one pair, so running pairs one after another
serializes 15 waves of a pool sized for 6 chunks. ``--null-sweep`` batches the
pairs themselves — ``--null-batch`` of them in flight at once, each still
dispatching one grader call per chunk, so the wire sees batch x chunks.

**``--null-batch`` defaults to 5.** Over six chunks that is 30 concurrent
dispatches, about three waves against fifteen for the serial version, and it
completed with no throttling when the six-chunk layout landed on 2026-08-08.
Batch size is a rate-limit tradeoff, not a correctness one, so lower it only
if a provider starts refusing. A failed pair does not sink the sweep: the
others finish, the failures are listed, and re-running the same command skips
whatever already landed against this bank and retries only the rest.

Change one identity field per rebuild. ``runner_effort`` is an identity field
just like ``grader`` is, so folding an ``--effort`` change into a grader or
chunk rebuild leaves two variables moving and no way to attribute the result.
"""

import sys
from pathlib import Path

# tools/ is not a package root, so put it on sys.path before importing score_evals.
sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))

from score_evals.cli import main

if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
